[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering BDDMockito: The Given-When-Then Syntax in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) encourages writing tests in a human-readable format, focusing on the behavior of the application rather than its implementation details. Mockito provides a dedicated syntax extension called BDDMockito, which replaces standard Mockito verification and stubbing methods with the familiar Given-When-Then structure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use BDDMockito?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improves test readability by aligning test code with business requirements using Given, When, and Then terminology."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Replaces confusing method names like when(...) with given(...), making stubbing read naturally in English."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Seamlessly integrates with existing JUnit and Mockito test suites."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparing Traditional Mockito vs BDDMockito"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In traditional Mockito, stubbing uses the when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value) pattern. While functional, it can sound unnatural when reading aloud. BDDMockito introduces given(mock.method()).willReturn(value) to solve this."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Traditional Mockito
when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(user));

// BDDMockito
given(userRepository.findById(1L)).willReturn(Optional.of(user));"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation Guide"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use BDDMockito, statically import methods from the BDDMockito class. Here is a complete example of testing an AuthenticationService using the Given-When-Then pattern."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.then;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.times;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.mockito.InjectMocks;
import org.mockito.Mock;
import org.mockito.junit.jupiter.MockitoExtension;

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
public class AuthenticationServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private TokenRepository tokenRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private AuthenticationService authService;

    @Test
    public void shouldReturnTrueWhenTokenIsValid() {
        // Given
        String token = "valid-token-123";
        given(tokenRepository.isTokenValid(token)).willReturn(true);

        // When
        boolean isValid = authService.authenticate(token);

        // Then
        assertThat(isValid).isTrue();
        then(tokenRepository).should(times(1)).isTokenValid(token);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Exceptions with BDDMockito"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Testing error flows is equally intuitive. Instead of thenThrow(), BDDMockito maps exception throwing to willThrow()."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void shouldThrowExceptionWhenDatabaseIsDown() {
    // Given
    String token = "any-token";
    given(tokenRepository.isTokenValid(token)).willThrow(new RuntimeException("Database connection failed"));

    // When & Then
    // Execute method and assert exception using AssertJ or JUnit
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"BDDMockito bridges the gap between technical unit testing and business requirements. By swapping when() with given() and verify() with then().should(), your test suites become dramatically easier to read, maintain, and share with non-technical stakeholders."}]
